        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oscillating tools are bought for awkward jobs, so we judged them on awkward jobs. The tasks that matter most are flush-cutting door casing and jambs so new flooring can slide underneath, plunge-cutting openings in drywall and subfloor, removing grout between tiles, trimming nails and pipe in tight spaces, and scraping old caulk and adhesive. A tool that handles those well will handle nearly anything a homeowner throws at it.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted oscillation angle, blade interface and vibration most heavily. These three factors, more than motor power or top speed, decide how fast a tool cuts, how accurately it plunges and how long you can use it before your hand goes numb. Top speed barely varies: almost every tool here peaks around 20,000 oscillations per minute, so it is a poor way to tell them apart.
          </p>

          <p>
            We included both cordless and corded tools because they suit different people. Cordless tools make sense for most homeowners, particularly those already on a battery platform. Corded tools still make sense for remodelers doing long cutting sessions and for people who use a multi-tool too rarely to keep batteries charged.
          </p>

          <p>
            We only list tools that are currently sold and widely available, and every spec on this page comes from the manufacturer. Where brands measure oscillation angle differently, as some list the swing to each side and others list the total, we have said so. We left out budget no-name tools because their blade clamps and bearings are the first things to fail, and a multi-tool with a slipping blade is worse than useless on a precision cut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. OPM and Oscillation Angle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oscillations per minute, or OPM, is the number of times the blade swings back and forth each minute. Nearly every tool on the market tops out between about 19,500 and 20,000 OPM, so top speed is not a meaningful differentiator. What matters more is the bottom of the range and how well the tool holds speed under load. A lower minimum, like the 8,000 OPM on the Bosch GOP55-36, gives more control on delicate work such as grout removal or scraping, and electronics that hold speed under load prevent the blade from stalling in dense material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oscillation angle is how far the blade swings from side to side, and it has the biggest effect on cutting speed. For years, 3.2 degrees total was the industry standard. Newer tools have pushed that to 3.6, 3.9 and even 4.2 degrees. A larger swing moves the teeth further on each stroke and clears sawdust from the cut more effectively, which means faster cuts and less burning in hardwood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful comparing angles across brands. Some manufacturers list the total swing, while others list the swing to each side. DeWalt, for example, lists 1.6 degrees for the DCS356, which corresponds to the 3.2-degree total standard. Fein lists 2 x 1.7 degrees for its MM 500 Plus, which is 3.4 degrees total. Always convert to total before comparing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">OSCILLATION ANGLE, TOTAL</p>
