            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A bigger angle is not always better. Larger swings produce more vibration and are harder to control on delicate work, which is why the best tools pair a large angle with good vibration control. For demolition, flush-cutting and plunge cuts in wood, a 3.6-degree or larger tool saves real time. For precision cuts in finished trim, a smaller angle and a lower speed are often easier to manage.
          </p>
        </div>

