        <div id="blade-systems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Starlock, StarlockPlus, StarlockMax and Universal Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade interfaces are the most confusing part of buying an oscillating tool, and they matter more than most buyers realize. Older tools used a flat interface with a pattern of pins or holes, clamped by a bolt or lever. Most tools now use a universal-fit design that accepts blades from most brands. Bosch and Fein developed the Starlock system, a three-dimensional star-shaped mount that grips the blade on all sides and transfers far more power without slipping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Starlock comes in three tiers, and compatibility only runs one way. Standard Starlock blades fit Starlock, StarlockPlus and StarlockMax tools, and many universal-fit tools too. StarlockPlus blades, designed for more powerful tools, fit StarlockPlus and StarlockMax tools only. StarlockMax blades, the heaviest-duty tier, fit only StarlockMax tools. So a StarlockMax tool like the Makita XMT04ZB or the Bosch GOP55-36 accepts every Starlock blade, while a universal-fit tool like the Milwaukee 2836 accepts only standard Starlock blades and universal blades.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Blade type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fits these tools</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Universal fit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most universal-fit tools, including Milwaukee, DeWalt, Ryobi</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General cutting, cheapest blades</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Starlock</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Starlock, StarlockPlus, StarlockMax and many universal tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light and medium cutting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">StarlockPlus</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">StarlockPlus and StarlockMax tools only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder materials, more powerful tools</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">StarlockMax</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">StarlockMax tools only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy remodeling and demolition</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Does it matter in practice? For occasional homeowner use, universal-fit blades work well and are cheaper and easier to find. For professionals and serious remodelers, a StarlockMax tool is worth it because the blade cannot slip under load, which improves plunge-cut accuracy and blade life. Either way, check the blade packaging before you buy: every Starlock blade is marked with the tier it belongs to.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical tip: some universal-fit blades are sold with an adapter for older tools, and a few tools ship with an adapter for OIS-style blades. Use the direct-fit blade where you can. Every adapter adds a bit of play at the blade, and play shows up as wandering cuts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blade-change" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tool-Free Blade Change
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every tool on this list has tool-free blade change, and you should not buy one that does not. Older multi-tools used a hex key to loosen a clamping bolt, which is slow and easy to lose. Modern tools use a lever or push-button release that lets you swap or reposition a blade in a few seconds. Since most jobs involve at least two different blades, such as a plunge-cut blade and a scraper, a fast change saves a surprising amount of time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better systems also let you reposition the blade at different angles without removing it. This matters more than it sounds. Flush-cutting a door jamb, reaching into a corner or cutting behind a pipe often requires the blade to sit at an angle to the tool body. A system with multiple angle positions lets you rotate the blade to suit the cut, rather than twisting your wrist into an awkward position.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some Starlock tools go a step further with a release that ejects the blade without touching it. This is genuinely useful because blades get very hot during use, particularly when cutting metal or removing grout. Being able to drop a hot blade into a bin without handling it is a safety feature as much as a convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the system, check that the blade is fully seated before starting a cut. A blade that is not properly locked will vibrate loose, wander off the line and wear the mount. With a Starlock tool you will feel a positive click. With a universal lever clamp, give the blade a firm tug before you start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Vibration and Comfort
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vibration is the part of an oscillating tool that spec sheets tend to skip, and it is what decides how long you can work accurately. A tool that buzzes hard through the grip numbs your fingers within a few minutes, and once your grip loosens, your cuts start to wander. For short jobs like trimming a single jamb this barely matters. For removing grout from a shower or sanding a large area, it matters more than anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better tools use two approaches to control vibration. Some isolate the motor and head from the grip with rubber mounts, as Milwaukee does on both its M18 and M12 tools. Others add an internal counterbalance that cancels out some of the oscillation, which is how Makita's Anti-Vibration Technology works. Both approaches help, and in our experience the difference between a well-damped tool and a basic one is obvious within a minute of use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grip shape matters too. A slim, small-diameter grip gives better control for most people and lets you hold the tool closer to the head, where precision is greatest. Heavy tools, especially corded models with a large motor, can become tiring when held at arm's length or overhead, which is where lighter cordless tools have an advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever tool you buy, you can reduce vibration by using sharp blades, letting the tool do the work without forcing it and choosing the right speed. Pushing too hard increases vibration and slows cutting. For long sessions, anti-vibration gloves and regular breaks help. Hearing protection is also a good idea, as the high-pitched whine of a multi-tool is more tiring than it seems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless-vs-corded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cordless vs Corded
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, a cordless oscillating tool is the better choice. Multi-tool jobs tend to be short and scattered across the house: trimming a jamb in one room, cutting a hole for an outlet in another, scraping caulk in the bathroom. Not having to find an outlet or drag a cord between rooms makes a real difference, and modern brushless cordless tools match or beat older corded tools for cutting speed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded tools still make sense in three situations. First, if you do long, sustained jobs like removing grout from a large tiled area or cutting out a whole subfloor, a corded tool never slows as the battery drains. Second, if you use a multi-tool only a few times a year, a corded tool avoids the frustration of finding a dead battery. Third, the most powerful StarlockMax tools, like the Bosch GOP55-36, are still corded tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime on cordless tools depends heavily on the battery. A compact 2.0Ah pack is fine for a few quick cuts but will not last through a grout-removal job. For heavy use, a 5.0Ah or larger battery is worth the extra weight. The same logic applies to other cordless tools: the batteries you buy for a multi-tool will also run your drill, saw and <a href="/best-impact-wrench/">impact wrench</a>, so it pays to invest in a few good packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a battery platform, buy the cordless multi-tool from that brand. If you do not, and you only need a multi-tool occasionally, a good corded tool will serve you well and costs less than buying into a new platform. If you plan to build a collection of cordless tools, choose the platform first and the multi-tool second.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="jobs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Common Jobs and How to Do Them
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flush-cutting door jambs and casing</h3>

          <p>
            The most common reason people buy an oscillating tool is to undercut door jambs and casing so new flooring can slide underneath. Lay a scrap of the new flooring and underlayment against the jamb as a guide, rest a flush-cut wood blade flat on top of it, and cut straight in. The blade removes exactly the right amount of material. Use a medium-high speed and let the blade do the work. Forcing it causes burning and a wandering cut. A larger oscillation angle makes a big difference here, since jamb stock is often dense hardwood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Removing grout</h3>

          <p>
            For grout removal, use a carbide grit or diamond grout blade sized to the width of the joint. Start at a lower speed to avoid chipping tile edges, and keep the blade centered in the joint. A tool with a low minimum speed, good vibration control and a long-lasting battery or cord is ideal. Take your time: grout removal is slow, and rushing it risks cracking tiles. Once the new grout has cured, seal it to prevent staining.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cutting drywall</h3>

          <p>
            Oscillating tools are excellent for cutting openings in drywall for outlets, switches and access panels, especially when working in existing walls. Use a drywall or wood blade and plunge-cut along your marked lines. The shallow cut depth reduces the risk of hitting wires or pipes, but always check before cutting. For finishing repaired drywall, a dedicated sander is faster than a multi-tool sanding pad; our guide to the <a href="/best-drywall-sander/">best drywall sander</a> covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other good uses include cutting nails and bolts flush, trimming copper and PVC pipe in tight spaces, scraping old caulk and adhesive, and detail sanding in corners. What an oscillating tool is not good at is long straight cuts or curves in sheet material. For those, a <a href="/best-cordless-circular-saw/">cordless circular saw</a> or a <a href="/best-jigsaw/">jigsaw</a> is far faster and more accurate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Blades Matter More Than the Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blade makes more difference to cutting performance than the tool itself. A cheap blade on a premium tool will cut slowly and wander, while a good blade on a budget tool will perform surprisingly well. The blades included with most tools are basic, and upgrading them is the single most cost-effective improvement you can make.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the blade to the material. High-carbon steel blades are cheap and work well in soft wood, drywall and plastic, but dull quickly on anything harder. Bimetal blades cut wood with embedded nails and thin metal and last much longer. Carbide-tooth blades are the best choice for hardwood, nail-embedded wood, fiberglass and heavy use, and they last many times longer than bimetal. Carbide grit and diamond blades are for grout, tile and masonry.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Spend on blades, not on top speed.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A bimetal or carbide blade on a mid-priced tool will outcut a basic high-carbon blade on the most expensive tool here. Buy a small assortment of quality blades for the jobs you actually do, and replace them when they dull rather than forcing them.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Blade shape matters too. Plunge-cut blades are straight and narrow, ideal for flush-cutting and cutting openings. Segmented or round blades cut in a curve and are good for long cuts in drywall and flush-cutting large areas. Scrapers remove caulk and adhesive. Sanding pads, usually triangular, reach into corners that other sanders cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace blades when they dull. A dull blade cuts slowly, generates heat, increases vibration and wears the tool. Forcing a dull blade through material is the most common cause of burned cuts and broken blades. For heavy use, buy blades in multipacks, which are much cheaper per blade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Choosing a Platform
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people end up with several cordless tools, so the battery platform matters more than any single tool. If you already own Milwaukee, DeWalt, Makita or Ryobi batteries, the best oscillating tool for you is almost certainly the best one on that platform. A bare tool is much cheaper than a kit, and your existing batteries will work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a platform from scratch, think about the other tools you will need. Milwaukee and DeWalt both have deep ranges for construction and automotive work, and our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> comparison covers where each is stronger. Makita is well regarded for woodworking tools and ergonomics, and Ryobi offers the widest range of affordable homeowner tools. Our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">best cordless tool brand</a> covers the full picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within a platform, consider whether you want a full-size or compact tool. Full-size 18V or 20V tools cut faster and run longer. Compact 12V tools are lighter and fit into tighter spaces. For most homeowners, a full-size tool is the better choice, but if much of your work is in cabinets, plumbing or tight corners, a compact tool may suit you better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy a few good blades with the tool. Whatever platform you choose, a small set of bimetal and carbide blades will do more for your results than any difference between the tools on this list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing oscillation angles without converting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some brands list the total swing and others list the swing to each side, so a 1.6-degree figure can mean the same as a 3.2-degree one. Always convert to the total angle before comparing tools. Otherwise you may think a tool is far less aggressive than it actually is, or pay extra for a difference that does not exist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying StarlockPlus or Max blades for a universal tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Starlock compatibility only runs one way. Standard Starlock blades fit most tools, but StarlockPlus blades need a StarlockPlus or StarlockMax tool, and StarlockMax blades only fit StarlockMax tools. Check the tier marked on the packaging before buying, or you will end up with blades that simply will not mount.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the included blade for everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The blades bundled with most tools are basic high-carbon steel and dull quickly on hardwood, nails or anything harder. Buy a few bimetal and carbide blades matched to the jobs you do. It is the cheapest way to make any oscillating tool cut faster, straighter and cooler, with less vibration.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing the tool through the cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pushing hard slows the blade, increases vibration, burns the wood and shortens blade life. Let the tool do the work with light, steady pressure, and rock the blade slightly on long plunge cuts to clear sawdust. If a cut is slow, the blade is probably dull or wrong for the material.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a multi-tool for long cuts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oscillating tools are built for short, awkward and precise cuts, not long straight cuts or curves in sheet material. Using one to rip plywood or cut a countertop is slow and inaccurate. Use a circular saw or jigsaw for long cuts, and save the multi-tool for the jobs nothing else can reach.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best oscillating tool for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, the best choice is the cordless oscillating tool from the battery platform you already own. If you are starting from scratch, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL 2836 is the best overall, while the Ryobi ONE+ HP PBLMT50 offers the best value. Both have tool-free blade change and brushless motors, and both accept widely available universal-fit blades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a larger oscillation angle really cut faster?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A larger oscillation angle moves the blade teeth further on each stroke and clears sawdust from the cut more effectively, which means noticeably faster cuts, especially in hardwood. The trade-off is more vibration and slightly less control on delicate cuts. Tools in the 3.6 to 4.2-degree range are faster than those at the old 3.2-degree standard.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will Starlock blades fit my oscillating tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard Starlock blades fit all Starlock tools and many universal-fit tools from other brands. StarlockPlus blades only fit StarlockPlus and StarlockMax tools, and StarlockMax blades only fit StarlockMax tools. Check your tool's manual or the manufacturer's website for its exact interface, and check the tier printed on the blade packaging before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an oscillating tool cut through nails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with the right blade. A bimetal or carbide blade will cut through nails, screws and thin metal, which makes an oscillating tool very useful for cutting through nail-embedded wood during remodeling. A high-carbon steel blade will dull almost immediately on metal. Use a moderate speed and let the blade do the work to avoid overheating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an oscillating tool good for removing grout?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is one of the best tools for the job. Use a carbide grit or diamond grout blade sized to the joint width, start at a low speed and keep the blade centered in the joint. Good vibration control and a long-lasting battery or a corded tool help, because grout removal is slow, sustained work. Take your time to avoid chipping tiles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a corded or cordless oscillating tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people should buy cordless, especially if they already own a battery platform, because multi-tool jobs are short and scattered around the house. Corded tools make sense for long, sustained jobs like large grout removal, for people who rarely use a multi-tool, and for those who want the most powerful StarlockMax models, which are still corded.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do oscillating tool blades last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the blade material and what you cut. High-carbon steel blades can dull after a few cuts in hardwood. Bimetal blades last much longer and handle nails. Carbide blades last many times longer than bimetal and are the best value for heavy use. Replace blades as soon as cutting slows or the wood starts to burn.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

